# Working notes for whoever picks this up next. Not documentation — the READMEs
# and docs/ are. This is the stuff that would otherwise have to be rediscovered.

test counts: 459 default / 512 with tls + cloud-http

## Advanced phases

completed:
- A15 Observability API: TrafficStats shared out of the dispatcher task,
  ClientRequest::Stats, NodeSummary enriched, same numbers on /metrics
- A16 TUI: crates/aether-tui (lib + bin), ratatui 0.29 with its re-exported
  crossterm. Verified by rendering real frames against a live 2-agent mesh;
  the README screenshots are those frames, not mock-ups.
- packaging: .github/workflows/release.yml builds all three binaries for
  6 targets on a v* tag. Distro packages (deb/brew/AUR) not done.

- A2 priority + queue: aether-core Priority (5 levels), controller queue.rs
  (linear scan, not a heap — it re-ranks on every pop, which ageing needs),
  run_dispatcher_with, QueueStats on MeshState, `priority` on Submit, TUI
  form field + queue panel. Live: 400 background tasks queued 381 deep, a
  critical submitted 250 ms in finished 57th of 401.

- A3 queue policies: max_size + Rejection (Reject / DropOldest /
  DropLowestPriority), queue-level and per-task deadlines, refused/expired
  counters. All off by default. Live: 300 tasks at 32 deep -> 42 ran, 258
  refused; unbounded with timeout_ms=200 -> 45 ran, 255 expired. Controller
  counters matched both exactly.

- A1 real-network benchmark: `aether-benchmark network` connects to a live
  controller as a client. bench/nodes.toml declares the mesh and the run is
  refused if it does not match. Report carries the environment and flags
  loopback-only. Live on 2 agents: 80 MiB naive vs 4 MiB, 95.0 %.
- aether-controller::connection: the client half of the protocol, shared by
  the TUI and the benchmark instead of being written twice.

- A25 reproducibility: reports carry the reproducing command (seed included,
  token never), client cpu count, RFC 3339 UTC (hand-rolled civil_from_days,
  tested against Python's calendar including 1900/2000/2024 leap rules).
  docs/benchmarks.md documents the procedure and the two conditions a rerun
  needs. Verified by rerunning a report's own command on a restarted mesh:
  identical bytes, wall clock within 3 %.

- A26 regression: `regress` compares a fresh run against bench/baseline.json
  and exits non-zero on a gated regression. CI job starts a real controller
  and two real agents and runs it on every push. Byte counts gate (arithmetic,
  verified stable across restarts/seeds/node counts); timings are advisory
  because a shared runner's wall clock is not. Verified by hand-editing a
  baseline to claim perfect dedup and watching it fail with exit 1.

- A8 workflow DAG: aether-core::workflow (validation, Kahn ordering, waves),
  TaskResult.output_id, agent keeps its output in its own store, controller
  records it in the catalog crediting that node, flow.rs runs the DAG, client
  API `workflow` request. No new transfer path and no new placement rule -
  dependency outputs become inputs and the existing locality score does the
  rest. Live on 3 nodes: 8 MiB chain, 0 bytes of intermediate data moved.

- A9 workflow locality: FlowResult carries per-step Placement (node, output
  size, inputs local vs moved, bytes moved), bytes_moved(), nodes_used().
  ScoreWeights is now serde + settable from controller config (the README had
  claimed configurable weights that were not reachable from the binary).

  MEASURED AND WRONG ABOUT THE CAUSE: a diamond concentrates on one node of
  three. I assumed the locality bonus. Ran it again with locality=0 and
  transfer=0 - identical placement. The real causes are (a) run_workflow
  dispatches one step at a time so nothing ever contends, and (b) load metrics
  lag a heartbeat so a node just given work does not look busier. Documented as
  limitations rather than tuned around.

- concurrent dispatch: TaskTransport takes &self; Controller's registry,
  manifests and a new in_flight map are shared; run_dispatcher spawns up to
  DEFAULT_IN_FLIGHT dispatches. registry_mut() is gone, replaced by
  register(&self, info).

  TWO bugs, and fixing one alone did nothing. (1) dispatch was serial. (2) the
  scheduler scored on heartbeat-reported load, so it could not see work it had
  just sent. With only (1) fixed: 64 tasks, all on one node of four, 1.0x
  parallelism. With both: 16/16/16/16, 3.8x, 166 -> 638 tasks/s.

  Verified the regression gate and examples/11-workflow both still hold: the
  traffic reduction is unchanged and a workflow still moves 0 bytes.

- concurrent workflow steps: run_workflow takes Arc<Controller> and spawns
  every ready step at once (Kahn, but concurrent). Per-step bytes_moved is
  gone - with steps overlapping, a counter sampled around one picks up
  another's transfers. FlowResult.bytes_moved is measured across the run
  instead; inputs_local/inputs_moved come from the catalog so they stay exact.

  This made the A9 locality knob mean something. Six branches on six nodes:
  locality=1.0 -> 1 node, 36 ms, 1.0x. locality=0.0 -> 4 nodes, 13 ms, 2.7x.
  In A9 that same comparison showed no difference at all, because there was
  never a second task in flight. Docs corrected in three places.

- A12/A13 checkpoint + resume: controller/checkpoint.rs. Journal (JSON lines,
  fsync per record), Record, fingerprint(&Workflow), Controller::with_checkpoint,
  flow::run_workflow_resumable, client API `run` field, config checkpoint_path.

  The journal holds step numbers and output ids, NEVER outputs. Local file
  only - no storage SDK, per the master prompt.

  Two guards, both tested: the workflow must match the fingerprint the run was
  recorded against (kind/payload/inputs/deps, task ids excluded because they
  are regenerated every submission), and the output must still be in the
  catalog. Only successful steps are recorded.

  KNOWN AND MEASURED LIMIT: a restarted controller resumes NOTHING. The journal
  survives; the catalog does not, so nothing is locatable and every step runs
  again. Verified by restarting with 2 records in the file: resumed=[]. Making
  restart work needs agents to re-announce their holdings on registration -
  that is a protocol change, deliberately not done here. Documented in the
  module doc and both READMEs rather than glossed.

  Live: 3-step chain, last step fails. Run 1 ran [0,1,2], journalled 2. Run 2
  ran [2], resumed [0,1]. A different workflow under the same name was refused
  naming both fingerprints. Unnamed run journalled nothing.

  Found in passing and fixed: StepOutcome.step was the position in the reply,
  not the step index, so any skipped step renumbered everything after it.
  FlowResult gained steps_run for exactly this.

- A10/A11 autoscale advisor: controller/autoscale.rs. Signals (nodes,
  queue_depth, oldest_wait, cpu_usage), Target (QueueLength / CpuUtilization /
  Latency), Policy, Decision carrying `why`, monitor() task driven by
  autoscale_interval_secs (0 = off, the default).

  It RECOMMENDS ONLY. It starts nothing and spends nothing, per the master
  prompt. Acting on a recommendation stays with aether-cloud or a person.

  Three things stop it being noise, and all three are tested: a tolerance dead
  band (without it a mesh at target recommends up/down forever - see
  a_mesh_does_not_oscillate_around_its_target), a cooldown, and a backlog veto
  on scale-down (low CPU + full queue means blocked, not idle).

  Live: one agent at --max-concurrent-tasks 1, 900 tasks, max_nodes=12,
  cooldown_secs=3 -> 0->1 (below minimum), then 1->12 at 667 queued, then
  1->12 at 166 queued three seconds later, then silence once drained.

- concurrent agent execution: handle_next spawns the task instead of awaiting
  it, bounded by a Semaphore (default = available_parallelism). Blocking on
  the permit is deliberate backpressure - a saturated node stops reading
  assignments. It cannot deadlock: nothing already running waits on a message,
  because the controller only dispatches after DataReady.
  One 16-core agent, 32 cpu tasks: 1.0 cores / 165 tasks/s at
  --max-concurrent-tasks 1, 9.2 cores / 1484 tasks/s at the default.

  All three serialisation points are now gone: controller dispatch, workflow
  steps, and agent execution.

next: A4 QUIC, A14 OTel, A20-A23 persistent state. Still nobody has run this on real hardware; that is now a nodes.toml and three machines away rather than a
harness away, which is as far as it can be closed from here.

not started: A1 real-network bench, A2/A3 priority queue (no queue exists at
all — submit dispatches immediately), A4 QUIC, A6 cost, A7 spot, A8/A9 DAG,
A14 OTel, A17-A23, A24-A26 bench

## Verified by actually running it, not just by tests

- three labelled agents + controller on this machine; examples/09 output in its
  README is real, including the refusal when nothing satisfies the constraints
- examples/10 (MeshExecutor) numbers are real, including the half where the mesh
  loses to a thread pool
- Java and C# SDKs compiled and run against a live mesh; their README output is
  what they printed
- /metrics and /healthz scraped from a running controller
- both sample TOML files loaded by the real binaries

## Things that cost time to find out

- reqwest 0.12 (rustls-tls), hmac 0.12, sha2 0.10 — newer majors changed APIs
- rcgen needs the x509-parser feature to sign with an existing CA
- bincode 2 renamed DeserializeError to DecodeError
- wasmtime's Store<T> requires 'static, so WASM inputs are Arc<[u8]> not &[u8]
- tokio's BufReader has no by_ref(); cap the reader with .take() instead
- PowerShell's Get-Content -Raw / -replace mangles UTF-8 em-dashes and can eat
  newlines. Use python or the Edit tool for source files.
- sysinfo's first CPU sample after construction is arbitrary, not 0.0 — on
  Windows it reports 100%. local_node_info sleeps one interval before sampling.
- cargo test -p aether-agent --all-features fails: it enables the agent's tls
  feature but not the controller's. Test the workspace, not the crate.
- Go toolchain lives in the scratchpad (not committed): go1.24.0 windows-amd64

## Deliberate design decisions worth not re-litigating

- the telemetry endpoint reports aggregates only; a per-node label would make an
  unauthenticated port into an inventory of the network (test pins this)
- a blob larger than the whole storage budget is stored anyway — refusing it
  would fail work the mesh already decided to run here
- heartbeat backoff is capped by the controller's declared eviction window, not
  by anything the agent chooses; an agent told nothing does not back off at all
- MeshExecutor refuses Python callables rather than running them locally
- cloud adapters read platform liveness only; utilisation comes from heartbeats

## Traps this repository has already fallen into

- the network benchmark first reported 100 %. The baseline's first task used
  the same seed as the shared dataset, so it published identical bytes and the
  second mode found them already on a node. Seeds are now disjoint by
  construction and a test pins it. If a benchmark number looks too good, it is.
- the same benchmark is not idempotent against a warm mesh: repeat a seed and
  the nodes still hold everything, so nothing moves. The seed defaults to
  something fresh and the report warns when the baseline moved nothing.

## Follow-ups deferred on purpose

- `run_dispatcher` still dispatches one task at a time. The queue makes that
  survivable rather than fixing it; concurrent dispatch needs Controller to
  stop being &mut-per-task.

- the 5 SDKs ignore the new NodeSummary fields (address, latency_ms,
  bandwidth, datasets_held, bytes_held, connected) and have no stats() call.
  Backward compatible, verified live. Mechanical to add; not this phase.

## Open work

- rebuild the catalog from agents on registration; without it checkpoint/resume
  cannot survive a controller restart, and neither can locality after one

- run any of this on real hardware over a real network (weakest claim here)
- run the cloud adapters against real accounts
- security review by someone who did not write it
- task priorities and queueing; placement is first-come, first-served
- QUIC transport
- cost-aware cross-region scheduling
